            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Bruce E. Johnson, petitioner\u2019s counsel argued that the patents are invalid because Congress created a distinct statutory scheme for plant varieties."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Edmund J. Sease",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a6f14db4cf364d5a31e7011feea2ff1d5627a46e5729cb0ddcec1c5da7d63219",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a6f14db4cf36-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 19,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Edmund J. Sease, respondent\u2019s counsel emphasized that the Plant Variety Protection statute was intended to protect the right to exclude others from sexually multiplying plant varieties, not to regulate seed marketing."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a6f14db4cf364d5a31e7011feea2ff1d5627a46e5729cb0ddcec1c5da7d63219",
